WEBVTT

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.620
Welcome to the Microsoft for Education Daily, Friday edition.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.180
Here's your day in Microsoft updates, in 3 minutes flat.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.530
Pause and question. In the Philippines,

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:22.070
2,000 public school teachers are set to earn AI literacy credentials.

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:26.110
It kicked off with about a hundred senior high teachers, hands-on, in Makati.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:36.700
Two online sessions follow this month, then eight live ones from January to May.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:42.920
And any teacher can join Microsoft Elevate for Educators,

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.790
and earn its free credential.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:47.590
To talk with teens,

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:52.530
there's Behind the Chat: three fictional young people in tricky AI situations.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:56.810
Microsoft's Madeline Shepherd says it's about helping young people pause,

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.280
and question what they see.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:00.720
Campus IT?

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:06.810
Microsoft detected about 7.3 billion email phishing threats from July to September.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.230
Many spoofed messages got through on broad security exceptions,

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:13.000
so it's worth checking yours.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.280
On campus devices,

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:21.380
Defender can now spot more than 50 local AI agents, on Windows and Mac.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:23.480
And in December,

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:27.810
Teams Rooms on Windows is due to show two shared screens side by side.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.560
Microsoft Research's PDD Kit turns your intent into a spec,

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.850
then proves an agent's code meets it.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:38.890
And for student coders,

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:43.430
GitHub now shows a project's accessibility statement on its overview page.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.030
The fine print? October's Windows update fixed over 1,100 flaws.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:51.520
So patch promptly.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.560
And from February,

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:57.930
admins should be able to make OneDrive and SharePoint share links expire.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:07.920
After hours? Xbox Insiders get a new PC app that launches up to 60% faster.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:14.860
History fans? Anno 117 is free for all Xbox members to try, until Sunday.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.710
And on supported smart TVs,

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:20.890
your phone can now be the controller for cloud games, in preview.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.250
Teachers learning AI, teens thinking twice,

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:29.150
proof-checked code: the smart move is to pause and question.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.820
Every day, the changelog gets a little longer.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.620
Some of it's shipped. The rest is on its way.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.880
One day, all of this. Tomorrow, probably more.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.950
That's the Microsoft for Education Daily for today.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:49.940
See you tomorrow.
